---
import Guide from '../layouts/Guide.astro';
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';
import CoverageCalculator from '../components/CoverageCalculator.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Is the suggested amount a price?',
    a: '<p>No. It’s the amount of coverage, the money paid to your family. Your monthly price depends on age, health answers, tobacco use and the amount you choose. <a href="/final-expense-insurance-cost/">See what changes the price</a>.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'Should I include cemetery costs?',
    a: '<p>If you want a burial, yes. The plot, opening and closing the grave, and a headstone are usually separate from the funeral home’s price. For cremation, include a niche or plot only if you want the ashes placed in a cemetery.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I buy more than $25,000?',
    a: '<p>Final expense plans usually stop around $25,000. If you need more, other kinds of life insurance can go higher. Call us and we’ll talk it through.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'What if prices go up by the time the money is needed?',
    a: '<p>Funeral prices tend to rise over time, which is why most families round up. The coverage amount on a final expense whole life policy normally stays the same, so a cushion helps.</p>',
  },
];
---
<Guide
  title="How Much Burial Insurance Do I Need? Calculator"
  h1="How much burial insurance do you need?"
  description="A quick calculator for burial and final expense coverage: add the funeral, cemetery costs and final bills, subtract savings, and see a suggested amount."
  path="/how-much-burial-insurance-do-i-need/"
  crumb="Coverage calculator"
  eyebrow="Calculator"
  lede="Add up the farewell you want and the bills you'd like to leave paid. The calculator does the math and suggests a coverage amount."
  summary={[
    "Start with the funeral or cremation, then add cemetery costs and final bills.",
    "Subtract any money already set aside for the funeral.",
    "Round up: funeral prices tend to rise over time.",
    "Most final expense plans offer about $5,000 to $25,000 of coverage.",
  ]}
>
  <CoverageCalculator />

  <h2>What goes into the number</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The funeral or cremation.</strong> The National Funeral Directors Association's 2023 price study put the national median at
      about $8,300 for a funeral with a viewing and burial, and about $6,280 with cremation. A direct cremation, with no viewing, usually costs
      much less.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Cemetery costs.</strong> A plot, opening and closing the grave, and a headstone are usually priced separately from the funeral home.</li>
    <li><strong>Final bills.</strong> Medical bills, credit cards, the last month's rent or utilities: anything you don't want to leave behind.</li>
    <li><strong>Money already set aside.</strong> Savings or an existing policy meant for the funeral lower the amount you need.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Prices vary a lot between providers. Under the FTC's Funeral Rule, funeral homes must give you an itemized price list, and tell you prices
    over the phone if you ask, so a couple of calls can make your estimate much more accurate.
  </p>

  <h2>Round up, but don't overbuy</h2>
  <p>
    The price of a policy climbs with each step up in coverage; $5,000 costs roughly half of $10,000. Choose enough to cover the plan you want
    with a cushion, and keep the monthly premium comfortable. No medical exam is needed. Approval depends on your answers to a few health
    questions.
  </p>

  <h2>Questions about coverage amounts</h2>
  <Faq items={faqs} />
</Guide>
